{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"AWS CloudTrail Enables Option to Add All Amazon S3 Buckets to Data Events","context":"AWS CloudTrail, a service that records API calls for usage analysis, now enables automatic addition of Amazon S3 buckets to S3 Data Events. Previously, users had to manually add each S3 bucket to track object-level operations, but now, with a few clicks, all new and existing S3 buckets can be logged. This feature is available in multiple regions including US, Canada, Asia Pacific, EU, and South America","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2017/09/aws-cloudtrail-enables-option-to-add-all-amazon-s3-buckets-to-data-events/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Uncover blind spots in AWS data plane operations with CloudTrail Event Coverage - AWS","context":"AWS CloudTrail's new feature, Event Coverage, allows users to view their data plane operations coverage at the account and organization levels in the AWS CloudTrail console. This feature helps identify gaps in logging posture by showing which AWS services and resource types have data event logging enabled and which do not. Data events enable tracking of data plane operations in AWS services, such as Amazon S3 object-level operations, to detect unauthorized access or exfiltration attempts. Event Coverage provides a centralized view of coverage across all supported data event sources and enables subscribing to data events directly from the dashboard. This simplifies closing coverage gaps, especially for organizations managing multiple accounts. Event Coverage is available in all commercial AWS Regions where AWS CloudTrail is supported. For more information on logging Data Events, visit the AWS CloudTrail documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/09/aws-cloudtrail-event-coverage/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"AWS CloudTrail Supports S3 Data Events","context":"AWS CloudTrail now supports Amazon S3 Data Events, enabling the recording of all API actions on S3 objects. This feature provides detailed information such as the AWS account of the caller, IAM user role, time of the API call, IP address, and other relevant details. Events are delivered to an S3 bucket and CloudWatch Events, allowing for programmatic actions on the events","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2016/11/aws-cloudtrail-supports-s3-data-events/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"AWS CloudTrail Adds Data Event Delivery to Amazon CloudWatch Logs","context":"AWS CloudTrail, a service that records API calls for security analysis, now delivers S3 data events to Amazon CloudWatch Logs. Previously, only management events were delivered to CloudWatch Logs. With this new feature, users can record detailed S3 object-level API activity, including the AWS account of the caller, IP address, and time of the API call. This enhancement enables the creation of alarms and notifications for actions like creating, modifying, or deleting files in S3 buckets","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2017/05/aws-cloudtrail-adds-data-event-delivery-to-amazon-cloudwatch-logs/"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region","skill_description":"Enables a multi-region AWS CloudTrail trail with S3 log storage, CloudWatch Logs integration, and CloudWatch Logs Insights queries for security monitoring and compliance auditing. Use when setting up centralized API activity logging across all AWS regions.\n\nServices: cloudtrail, s3, cloudwatch, iam, kms\nTasks: configure, secure, monitor\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: security, monitoring\n*","skill_name":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify CloudTrail S3 data event selector ARN wildcard behavior in docs"}

{"id": "d1856c57-8b95-48b9-b799-3403b5ca20fa", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "This confirms the "select all S3 buckets" console option maps to setting Values to "arn:aws:s3" without a bucket name in basic event selectors, which I recall is documented in the classic CloudTrail docs.

Working through the logic: the all-buckets wildcard means logs-a, data-b, and data-c all have data events logged, assuming they're in the same account as the trail. Since there's no DataResource entry for Lambda functions in the event selectors, fn-x and fn-y only get management events logged, not data-plane Invoke events. I'll note the caveat about account scope and present this answer directly.

